<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple in Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Second Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> was the central sanctuary of ancient Jewish worship. After the first temple was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, the Achaemenid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> permitted exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to return and rebuild it, and the Second Temple was completed in the late sixth century BCE. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> cite this as an example of Persian tolerance towards subject peoples' religions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Temple in Jerusalem" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_Modell_BW_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, the First Temple was built by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> in the 10th century BCE, completed in 957 BCE. As the sole place of Israelite sacrifice, according to Deuteronomy, it replaced the Tabernacle built in the Sinai under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> says it was sacked a few decades later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shishak</a>, identified as the pharaoh Shoshenq I. It was destroyed c. 587 BCE in the Babylonian siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Archaeological excavations have not found remnants of it.</p><p>According to the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> called for the Second Temple to be built, and work began in 538 BCE. Some 19th-century calculations place completion on 21 February 515 BCE, though some modern researchers consider these dates unreliable. The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerubbabel">Zerubbabel</a> dedicated the new temple, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> later renovated it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The First Temple had an outer court where people assembled, an inner court of the priests and the temple building, with the larger Holy Place and the smaller inner sanctum, the Holy of Holies. The Herodian Temple was the most elaborate, with a wide precinct including the Court of the Gentiles, the Court of the Women and the Court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> for ritually pure Jewish men. Two surviving pieces of Herod's expansion are the Temple Warning inscriptions, which forbade pagans to enter the courtyard, and the Trumpeting Place inscription.</p><p>Daily morning and afternoon offerings were made there, with special offerings on festivals, while Levites recited Psalms. The daily service included the Shema and the Priestly Blessing, which still form the basis of the traditional morning service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The destruction of the Temple is mourned on the fast day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisha-bav">Tisha B'Av</a>, and weddings traditionally end with the groom breaking a glass in remembrance. Daily prayer contains petitions for its rebuilding. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the Temple appeared in illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, marriage contracts and wedding rings.</p><p>For Muslims the site is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">Al-Aqsa Mosque</a> compound, or Haram al-Sharif, regarded as the third-holiest site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the destination of Muhammad's Night Journey. According to tradition, Umar cleared the site after the Muslim conquest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> was built on the mount under Abd al-Malik. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, Matthew 24:2 records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> predicting the destruction of the Second Temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temple in Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cyrus Cylinder has been celebrated in modern times as an early articulation of humane rule, and the Achaemenid treatment of the Jewish exiles in Babylonia, allowing the rebuilding of the <b>Temple in Jerusalem</b>, had lasting consequences for the development of Judaism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jeroboam ruled first from Shechem and later from Tirzah, and he set up royal sanctuaries at Bethel and Dan, each with a golden calf, so that his subjects need not go on pilgrimage to the <b>Temple in Jerusalem</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Seleucid confrontation with the Maccabean revolt in Judaea (167–142 BCE), triggered by Antiochus IV Epiphanes's desecration of the <b>Jerusalem Temple</b> and attempted suppression of Jewish religious practice, produced the independent Hasmonean state, commemorated in the festival of Hanukkah, whose politics and religion formed the world into which Christianity…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bible describes Solomon dividing Israel into twelve administrative districts, each supplying the court for one month a year, and imposing corvée labour for building projects, including the <b>Temple of Jerusalem</b> and his palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…said by Prosper of Aquitaine to have begged Gaiseric to be content with pillage, carried off countless valuables, among them the spoils of the <b>Temple in Jerusalem</b> brought to Rome by Titus, and the empress Licinia Eudoxia and her daughters, one of whom, Eudocia, married Huneric at Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the following centuries, under Persian, Greek and then Roman rule, the scriptures were gathered and edited and the religion of the <b>Second Temple</b> period developed, with its priests, its scribes and its rival schools: the Pharisees, the Sadducees, the Essenes and others.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
